„­bible_version£KJV¤book+§chapter¨sections—…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙEThen Agrippa said unto Paul, Thou art permitted to speak for thyself.¦layout ‚¤textÙ=Then Paul stretched forth the hand, and answered for himself:¦layout©paragraphªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”I think myself happy, king Agrippa, because I shall answer for myself this day before thee touching all the things whereof I am accused of the Jews:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‘especially because I know thee to be expert in all customs and questions which are among the Jews: wherefore I beseech thee to hear me patiently.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnMy manner of life from my youth, which was at the first among mine own nation at Jerusalem, know all the Jews;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwhich knew me from the beginning, if they would testify, that after the most straitest sect of our religion I lived a Pharisee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWAnd now I stand and am judged for the hope of the promise made of God unto our fathers:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ—unto which promise our twelve tribes, instantly serving God day and night, hope to come. For which hope's sake, king Agrippa, I am accused of the Jews.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUWhy should it be thought a thing incredible with you, that God should raise the dead?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verse	ªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgI verily thought with myself, that I ought to do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth.¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
