„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ And I punished them oft in every synagogue, and compelled them to blaspheme; and being exceedingly mad against them, I persecuted them even unto strange cities.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙUWhereupon as I went to Damascus with authority and commission from the chief priests,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’at midday, O king, I saw in the way a light from heaven, above the brightness of the sun, shining round about me and them which journeyed with me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÁAnd when we were all fallen to the earth, I heard a voice speaking unto me, and saying in the Hebrew tongue, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? it is hard for thee to kick against the pricks.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙNAnd I said, Who art thou, Lord? And he said, I am Jesus whom thou persecutest.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙİBut rise, and stand upon thy feet: for I have appeared unto thee for this purpose, to make thee a minister and a witness both of these things which thou hast seen, and of those things in the which I will appear unto thee;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRdelivering thee from the people, and from the Gentiles, unto whom now I send thee,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÕto open their eyes, and to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan unto God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins, and inheritance among them which are sanctified by faith that is in me.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙJWhereupon, O king Agrippa, I was not disobedient unto the heavenly vision:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇbut shewed first unto them of Damascus, and at Jerusalem, and throughout all the coasts of JudÃ¦a, and then to the Gentiles, that they should repent and turn to God, and do works meet for repentance.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMFor these causes the Jews caught me in the temple, and went about to kill me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºHaving therefore obtained help of God, I continue unto this day, witnessing both to small and great, saying none other things than those which the prophets and Moses did say should come:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ–that Christ should suffer, and that he should be the first that should rise from the dead, and should shew light unto the people, and to the Gentiles.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd as he thus spake for himself, Festus said with a loud voice, Paul, thou art beside thyself; much learning doth make thee mad.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_But he said, I am not mad, most noble Festus; but speak forth the words of truth and soberness.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ±For the king knoweth of these things, before whom also I speak freely: for I am persuaded that none of these things are hidden from him; for this thing was not done in a corner.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFKing Agrippa, believest thou the prophets? I know that thou believest.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIThen Agrippa said unto Paul, Almost thou persuadest me to be a Christian.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ™And Paul said, I would to God, that not only thou, but also all that hear me this day, were both almost, and altogether such as I am, except these bonds.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjAnd when he had thus spoken, the king rose up, and the governor, and Bernice, and they that sat with them:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzand when they were gone aside, they talked between themselves, saying, This man doeth nothing worthy of death or of bonds.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkThen said Agrippa unto Festus, This man might have been set at liberty, if he had not appealed unto CÃ¦sar.¦layout ªlast_verse 